From: Mostly_Harmless <geek520170@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:30:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] fix(ass): do not read past a trailing "&#" in assEscape

For text ending in "&#" the emoji branch skipped a ';' that does not
exist and moved srcPtr past the terminating NUL, then kept copying
whatever followed in memory. With XML input such as "abc&amp;#" the
stale bytes of the unescaped buffer ("p;#") ended up in the subtitle;
with an exactly sized buffer it is a heap-buffer-overflow read. Only
skip the delimiter when there is one; all other inputs are unchanged.
---
 src/AssFile/AssStringProcessing.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/AssFile/AssStringProcessing.c b/src/AssFile/AssStringProcessing.c
index e59cc02..e8598bf 100644
--- a/src/AssFile/AssStringProcessing.c
+++ b/src/AssFile/AssStringProcessing.c
@@ -330,7 +330,9 @@ char *assEscape(char *dstStr, char *srcStr, int dstStrLen, int mode)
                     strGetLeftPart(emojiUnicodeText, &srcCmpPtr, ';', 8);
                     unicode_to_utf8(atoi(emojiUnicodeText), utf8Hex);
                     assEscapedTextPtr = utf8Hex;
-                    srcPtr += strlen(emojiUnicodeText) + 1; // "128514" + ";"
+                    // "128514" + ";". A trailing "&#" has nothing after it to skip:
+                    // stepping over its terminator would read past the string.
+                    srcPtr += strlen(emojiUnicodeText) + (srcPtr[2] != '\0');
                 }
 
                 /* 拷贝转义后字串 */
